{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785702263,"nonce":"WsBXEqMJAAoIT783Uhelsrz6kjI=","title":"Promoted from \"deferred candidate\" to **the next ADR** on 2026-08-03 (grill; ADR-0066), gated on the cold-flush measurement as a SIZING input rather than a go/no-go.","message":"**S3 has no batch PUT.** `DeleteObjects` batches deletes; S3 Batch Operations is a managed job over an existing inventory. One object, one request, is the floor — so the only lever is packing many blobs into one object with an index.\n\n**The insight that promoted it: laziness was the only reason to address blobs individually.** With eager materialisation every read is \"give me this whole snapshot\" — `/flat` already returns the manifest in one call and we then make 50k separate requests to fetch what it describes. That is a pack-shaped access pattern served by a per-object store. Packing's main downside (you cannot cheaply fetch one blob in isolation) is a cost we no longer pay for anything, and packing helps BOTH legs: the flush becomes one PUT instead of 20k, the feed a handful of ranged GETs instead of 50k.\n\n**The costs this ADR must carry explicitly — they are why it is a separate ADR:**\n- **A durable global `hash -\u003e (pack, offset, len)` index**, realistically Postgres (an S3 index object is a write hotspot with a lost-update problem once N replicas flush concurrently). This LOSES the property that the bucket is self-describing and a fresh Depot pointed at it just works; recovery means rebuilding from every pack's footer.\n- **GC becomes REPACKING** — a 5000-blob pack with 4000 dead blobs cannot be partially deleted. That is `git gc` / restic `prune`, with an index swap that must be atomic against concurrent readers.\n- **Append-only immutability is lost.** Nothing rewrites objects today, so a bug cannot corrupt existing data. Repacking rewrites.\n- **Read efficiency depends on LOCALITY** — a snapshot sharing 90% of its content with an older one needs blobs scattered across many packs unless we repack in reachability order, as git does.\n- **The bucket becomes opaque** to `aws s3 ls`.\n\n**It gives back:** the presence index becomes free — \"do we have this blob?\" is a local index lookup instead of a network HEAD, which subsumes the near-term cheap win and removes the whole probe pass.\n\n**Shape if adopted:** hybrid — pack small blobs, store large ones directly. Matches the measurement (cost tracks file COUNT, and CI trees are dominated by small files).\n\nThis changes the CAS's frozen canonical on-disk form, which carries a cross-binary skew tripwire — it needs its own migration story.\n\n- [ ] Cold-flush measured first (sibling ticket)\n- [ ] ADR written with the index, repacking and locality costs carried explicitly\n- [ ] Migration story for existing buckets","files":null}]}